        <definition>imp_general.comparisons.get_components_placement_scores</definition>
        <argsstring>(assembly, native_assembly, align=False)</argsstring>
        <name>get_components_placement_scores</name>
        <qualifiedname>imp_general.comparisons.get_components_placement_scores</qualifiedname>
        <param>
          <type>assembly</type>
          <defname>assembly</defname>
        </param>
        <param>
          <type>native_assembly</type>
          <defname>native_assembly</defname>
        </param>
        <param>
          <type>align</type>
          <defname>align</defname>
          <defval>False</defval>
        </param>
        <briefdescription>
<para>Compute the placement score of each of the children of an assembly. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>The function does not do any time of alignment of the coordinates <parameterlist kind="param"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>assembly</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>An IMP.atom.Molecule object </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>native_assembly</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>An IMP.atom.Molecule object with the native conformation. Obviously the atoms in assembly and native assembly must be the same </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>align</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>If True, assembly is aligned to native_assembly before calculating the placement score </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
<simplesect kind="return"><para>The function returns 2 lists. The first list contains the placement distances of the children. The second list contains the placement angles</para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="note"><para>This function is only available in Python. </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
        </detaileddescription>
